window.docContents[126].resolve({"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___6___-Characters-of-representations/Linear-independence-of-characters_SEMI_-basis-in-semisimple-case/#Chapter3___Theorem3___6___2":{"contents":"Theorem 3.6.2. (i) Characters of (distinct) irreducible finite dimensional representations of A are linearly independent.\n\n(ii) If A is a finite dimensional semisimple algebra, then these characters form a basis of (A/[A, A])^*.\n\nProof. (i) If V_1, \\ldots, V_r are nonisomorphic irreducible finite dimensional representations of A, then the map\n\n\\rho_{V_1} \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus \\rho_{V_r} : A \\to \\operatorname{End} V_1 \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus \\operatorname{End} V_r\n\nis surjective by the density theorem, so \\chi_{V_1}, \\ldots, \\chi_{V_r} are linearly independent. (Indeed, if \\sum \\lambda_i \\chi_{V_i}(a) = 0 for all a \\in A, then \\sum \\lambda_i \\operatorname{Tr}(M_i) = 0 for all M_i \\in \\operatorname{End}_k V_i. But each \\operatorname{Tr}(M_i) can range independently over k, so it must be that \\lambda_1 = \\cdots = \\lambda_r = 0.)\n\n(ii) First we prove that [\\operatorname{Mat}_d(k), \\operatorname{Mat}_d(k)] = \\mathfrak{sl}_d(k), the set of all matrices with trace 0. It is clear that [\\operatorname{Mat}_d(k), \\operatorname{Mat}_d(k)] \\subseteq \\mathfrak{sl}_d(k). If we denote by E_{ij} the matrix with 1 in the ith row of the jth column and 0's everywhere else, we have [E_{ij}, E_{jm}] = E_{im} for i \\neq m and [E_{i,i+1}, E_{i+1,i}] = E_{ii} - E_{i+1,i+1}. Now \\{E_{im}\\} \\cup \\{E_{ii} - E_{i+1,i+1}\\} forms a basis in \\mathfrak{sl}_d(k), so indeed [\\operatorname{Mat}_d(k), \\operatorname{Mat}_d(k)] = \\mathfrak{sl}_d(k), as claimed.\n\nBy semisimplicity, we can write A = \\operatorname{Mat}_{d_1}(k) \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus \\operatorname{Mat}_{d_r}(k). Then [A, A] = \\mathfrak{sl}_{d_1}(k) \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus \\mathfrak{sl}_{d_r}(k), and A/[A, A] \\cong k^r. By Theorem\n3.3.1, there are exactly r irreducible representations of A (isomorphic to k^{d_1}, \\ldots, k^{d_r}, respectively) and therefore r linearly independent characters on the r-dimensional vector space A/[A, A]. Thus, the characters form a basis. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory\t3.6. Characters of representations\tLinear independence of characters; basis in semisimple case","header":"Linear independence of characters; basis in semisimple case","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___6___-Characters-of-representations/Linear-independence-of-characters_SEMI_-basis-in-semisimple-case/#Chapter3___Theorem3___6___2"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Representations-of-quivers-as-representations-of-path-algebras/#Chapter2___Discussion_path_algebra_intro":{"contents":"It turns out that the theory of representations of quivers is a part of the theory of representations of algebras in the sense that for each quiver Q, there exists a certain algebra P_Q, called the path algebra of Q, such that a representation of the quiver Q is \"the same\" as a representation of the algebra P_Q. We shall first define the path algebra of a quiver and then justify our claim that representations of these two objects are \"the same\".\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.8. Quivers\tRepresentations of quivers as representations of path algebras","header":"Representations of quivers as representations of path algebras","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Representations-of-quivers-as-representations-of-path-algebras/#Chapter2___Discussion_path_algebra_intro"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/Characters-and-tensor-products-for-Heisenberg-group/#Chapter4___Problem4___12___9":{"contents":"Problem 4.12.9. Find the characters and tensor products of irreducible complex representations of the Heisenberg group from Problem 4.12.2.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\t4.12. Problems\tCharacters and tensor products for Heisenberg group","header":"Characters and tensor products for Heisenberg group","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/Characters-and-tensor-products-for-Heisenberg-group/#Chapter4___Problem4___12___9"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Section-6___1-heading___-Problems/#Chapter6___Section6___1_heading___heading-1":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.1. Problems\tSection 6.1 heading: Problems","header":"6.1. Problems","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Section-6___1-heading___-Problems/#Chapter6___Section6___1_heading___heading-1"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Section-9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___3":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t9. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\t9.3. The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra\tSection 9.3: The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra","header":"Section 9.3: The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Section-9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___3"}});
